-- Q1: medium / L3 — Dividends payable: effect of declaration on liabilities and equity
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Stern Corp.''s board of directors declares a $2.50 per share cash dividend on December 15, 2025, to shareholders of record on December 28, payable on January 15, 2026. Stern has 40,000 shares outstanding. What is the most likely effect of the declaration on Stern''s December 31, 2025 balance sheet?', '["Total liabilities decrease by $100,000 and equity is unchanged", "Total assets decrease by $100,000 and equity decreases by $100,000", "Total liabilities increase by $100,000 and retained earnings decrease by $100,000", "No effect until the January 15 payment date"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 505-20, a cash dividend creates a liability on the declaration date, not the payment date. On December 15, Stern debits retained earnings and credits dividends payable for $100,000 (40,000 × $2.50), increasing current liabilities and reducing equity. Choice D is incorrect because the obligation arises when the board formally commits to the distribution, not when cash is disbursed.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Customer refund liability under ASC 606
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Cascade Outfitters sells apparel with a 60-day return policy. In Q4 2025, the company sells $1,200,000 in merchandise. At December 31, the return period remains open on $800,000 of those sales and Cascade estimates a 5% return rate based on historical data. Analyze how the estimated returns affect Cascade''s year-end financial statements.', '["Revenue is reduced by $60,000 with no liability recognized", "A refund liability of $40,000 is recorded and a corresponding right-of-return asset is recognized", "A refund liability of $60,000 is recorded with no corresponding asset", "Revenue is reduced by $40,000 and all returns are recognized as a selling expense"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 606-10-32-10, when a right of return exists, the entity recognizes a refund liability for expected returns and a corresponding asset for the right to recover products. The refund liability applies to the $800,000 still within the return window: $800,000 × 5% = $40,000. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 606 requires both the refund liability and the return asset, not just the liability alone.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Sales tax payable definition
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'What type of account is sales tax collected from customers but not yet remitted to the government?', '["A current liability", "A revenue account", "A contra-revenue account", "An operating expense"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sales tax collected is held in trust for the taxing authority and reported as a current liability under ASC 210-10-45. The amount does not belong to the entity and is not revenue. Choice B is incorrect because the entity acts as a collection agent — the tax is a pass-through obligation owed to the government, not earned income from operations.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Contingent consideration remeasurement (ASC 805)
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Ridgemont Holdings acquired Silverline Corp. on March 1, 2025, agreeing to pay an additional $500,000 if Silverline''s Year 1 revenue exceeds $3,000,000. At acquisition, the fair value of this contingent consideration was $320,000, classified as a liability. By December 31, 2025, Silverline''s revenue trajectory indicates a 90% probability of exceeding the target and the fair value is now $450,000. The CFO proposes recording the $130,000 increase as an adjustment to goodwill. Evaluate the correct treatment of the remeasurement.', '["Record the $130,000 increase as an adjustment to goodwill because it relates to an acquisition", "No adjustment is needed until the contingency is resolved with certainty", "Record the $130,000 increase partly in goodwill and partly in earnings", "Record the $130,000 increase as a loss in current-period earnings"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 805-30-35-1, contingent consideration classified as a liability is remeasured at fair value each reporting period, with changes recognized in earnings — not as a goodwill adjustment. The $130,000 increase ($450,000 − $320,000) is a current-period loss. Choice A is incorrect because goodwill adjustments for contingent consideration are prohibited outside the measurement period and within it only for new information about acquisition-date facts.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Current maturities of long-term debt
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'What is the current portion of long-term debt?', '["The total principal balance of all outstanding notes payable", "The amount of long-term debt principal due within one year of the balance sheet date", "Accrued interest on long-term debt as of the balance sheet date", "The unamortized discount on bonds payable"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 470-10-45, the current portion of long-term debt is the principal amount maturing within one year (or the operating cycle, if longer) of the balance sheet date, reclassified from noncurrent to current liabilities. Choice A is incorrect because only the portion due within the next year is reclassified — the remaining principal stays in noncurrent liabilities.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Bonus accrual effect on financial statements
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Prescott Manufacturing has a management bonus plan that pays 10% of net income before bonuses to eligible executives. Preliminary net income before the bonus is $1,500,000 for 2025. The bonus is approved by the compensation committee on December 20, 2025, and will be paid on February 28, 2026. Analyze the effect on Prescott''s December 31, 2025 financial statements.', '["Compensation expense increases by $150,000 and accrued liabilities increase by $150,000", "No effect until payment on February 28, 2026", "Compensation expense increases by $150,000 and cash decreases by $150,000", "Retained earnings decrease by $150,000 with no liability recognized"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 710-10-25, an employer must accrue compensation when the obligation is attributable to services already rendered, payment is probable, and the amount is reasonably estimable. Prescott records $150,000 ($1,500,000 × 10%) as compensation expense and accrued bonus payable at year-end. Choice B is incorrect because the expense relates to 2025 services and was approved before year-end, satisfying all accrual criteria regardless of the cash payment date.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Assurance-type vs service-type warranty (ASC 606)
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Whitfield Appliances sells dishwashers with a standard 2-year warranty covering defects and an optional 3-year extended warranty sold separately for $120. In 2025, the company sold 8,000 dishwashers at $650 each and 3,200 extended warranties. Analyze how the two warranty types differ in financial statement treatment.', '["Both warranties are accrued as liabilities using estimated costs at the time of sale", "Both warranties are treated as separate performance obligations with revenue deferred", "The standard warranty is accrued as a cost under ASC 460 and the extended warranty is a separate performance obligation with deferred revenue under ASC 606", "The standard warranty is expensed as incurred and the extended warranty is accrued as a liability"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 606-10-25-1, an assurance-type warranty covering defects is not a separate performance obligation — the entity accrues estimated costs as a liability under ASC 460. A service-type warranty sold separately is a distinct performance obligation, and the $120 price is deferred as unearned revenue recognized over the coverage period. Choice A is incorrect because it fails to distinguish the revenue deferral required for the extended warranty.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — Exit/disposal obligations: multi-factor evaluation (ASC 420)
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Canton Industries announces a restructuring plan on November 1, 2025, to close its Eastern division by March 31, 2026. The plan includes: (1) one-time involuntary termination benefits of $280,000 for 35 employees communicated on December 1, (2) an operating lease with 18 months remaining and no economic benefit at $15,000 monthly rent, and (3) estimated equipment relocation costs of $90,000. Evaluate which items should be accrued at December 31, 2025.', '["Accrue all three items totaling $640,000 because the plan was formally approved", "Accrue termination benefits of $280,000 once communicated and the lease cease-use liability, but do not accrue relocation costs", "Accrue only the termination benefits because lease and relocation costs are future period expenses", "Accrue the lease liability and relocation costs but defer termination benefits until employees are terminated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 420-10-25, one-time termination benefits are recognized when communicated to employees (December 1 here). The operating lease cease-use liability is recognized when the entity stops using the space (ASC 420-10-25-15). However, ASC 420-10-25-20 explicitly prohibits accruing relocation costs — they are expensed as incurred. Choice A is incorrect because relocation costs are never part of an exit obligation accrual under ASC 420, even when included in a formal restructuring plan.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Payroll tax liabilities: employer portion calculation
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Hargrove Consulting has a biweekly payroll of $180,000 ending December 26, 2025. The employer''s share of FICA is 7.65% and all employees are below the Social Security wage base. Federal and state unemployment taxes total $2,400. What total employer payroll tax liability should Hargrove accrue for this pay period?', '["$13,770", "$11,370", "$16,800", "$16,170"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 712-10, the employer accrues its share of payroll taxes when employees earn wages. Employer FICA = $180,000 × 7.65% = $13,770, plus unemployment taxes of $2,400, totaling $16,170. Choice A ($13,770) is incorrect because it omits the federal and state unemployment tax obligations, which are also employer liabilities that must be accrued when wages are earned.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Accrued utilities: period-end cutoff
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Dalton Fabrication''s electricity meter reading indicates December 2025 consumption of approximately $14,200, but the utility invoice will not arrive until January 12, 2026. What adjusting entry should Dalton record at December 31, 2025?', '["Debit Utilities Expense $14,200 and credit Accrued Liabilities $14,200", "Debit Utilities Expense $13,800 and credit Accrued Liabilities $13,800", "No entry is required until the invoice is received in January", "Debit Prepaid Utilities $14,200 and credit Cash $14,200"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under the accrual basis of accounting (ASC 405-20), expenses must be recognized in the period incurred regardless of when invoiced. Dalton accrues the $14,200 estimated consumption as utilities expense with a corresponding accrued liability. Choice C is incorrect because waiting for the invoice would understate both expenses and liabilities for the period in which the electricity was consumed.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Customer deposits as liability
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'How should a refundable deposit received from a customer be classified on the balance sheet?', '["As revenue in the income statement", "As a reduction of accounts receivable", "As a current liability", "As deferred income in equity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 210-10-45, refundable deposits represent obligations to return cash to customers and are classified as current liabilities (or noncurrent if the refund period extends beyond one year). The deposit is not earned revenue because the entity has a present obligation to return the funds. Choice A is incorrect because no goods or services have been exchanged — the deposit remains an obligation until earned or refunded.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Loyalty program points as performance obligation (ASC 606)
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Summit Grocery operates a loyalty program granting 1 point per dollar spent, redeemable at 500 points for a $5 discount. In Q4 2025, customers earn 2,000,000 points on $2,000,000 in purchases and Summit estimates a 70% redemption rate. Analyze the effect of the loyalty program on Q4 revenue recognition.', '["Revenue of $2,000,000 is recognized with no adjustment for the loyalty program", "Revenue is reduced by $14,000 and a contract liability is recorded for the estimated redemption value", "Revenue is reduced by $20,000 and a promotional expense is recorded", "The loyalty program is disclosed in footnotes but does not affect revenue recognition"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 606-10-55-42, loyalty points represent a material right and a separate performance obligation. The standalone selling price is allocated from the transaction price: 2,000,000 points ÷ 500 × $5 = $20,000 face value, adjusted for 70% expected redemption = $14,000 contract liability reducing recognized revenue. Choice C is incorrect because loyalty points are not a selling expense — they are a performance obligation requiring revenue allocation under ASC 606.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Royalty payable accrual
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Pinecrest Publishing licenses a textbook from an author, paying a royalty of 12% on net sales. Net sales for Q4 2025 total $425,000 and the royalty is payable within 60 days after quarter-end. What amount should Pinecrest accrue as royalty payable at December 31, 2025?', '["Accrue $51,000 as royalty payable", "Accrue $42,500 as royalty payable", "Accrue $25,500 as royalty payable", "Defer accrual until the payment deadline"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 928-340 and the matching principle, royalty expense is recognized in the period the underlying sales occur. Pinecrest accrues $425,000 × 12% = $51,000 as royalty payable at December 31, regardless of the 60-day payment term. Choice D is incorrect because the obligation arose when Q4 sales were made — the payment deadline does not defer expense recognition under accrual accounting.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Income tax payable definition
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'What does income tax payable represent on the balance sheet?', '["The deferred tax liability arising from temporary differences", "The cumulative tax expense since the entity''s inception", "The estimated future tax on unrealized gains", "The amount of income tax owed to taxing authorities for the current period that has not yet been paid"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 740-10-25, income tax payable is a current liability representing taxes owed on the current period''s taxable income that remain unpaid at the balance sheet date. It differs from deferred tax liabilities, which arise from temporary differences between book and tax bases. Choice A is incorrect because deferred tax liabilities are noncurrent items reflecting future tax consequences, not amounts currently due.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Retention payable in construction
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'What is a retention payable in the construction industry?', '["An amount withheld from progress billings to a contractor until project completion or satisfaction of contract terms", "A penalty assessed for late delivery of materials", "An advance payment made to secure contractor availability", "A performance bond posted by the general contractor"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 606-10-45-3 and industry practice, retention (retainage) payable represents amounts withheld from contractor billings — typically 5% to 10% — until specified milestones or project completion. The amount is a liability of the entity withholding it. Choice C is incorrect because advance payments are assets (prepayments), not withheld amounts owed to a contractor for work already performed.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: hard / L3 — Operating lease liability: current vs noncurrent classification
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Waverly Hospitality enters a 10-year operating lease on January 1, 2025, with annual payments of $96,000 due each December 31. The discount rate is 5%. After making the first payment on December 31, 2025, the remaining lease liability is $595,340. The present value of the December 31, 2026 payment at 5% is $91,429. Analyze the proper classification of the lease liability on the December 31, 2025 balance sheet.', '["The entire $595,340 is classified as noncurrent because the lease term exceeds 12 months", "The full $595,340 is classified as current because annual payments are due each December 31", "Current liability of $91,429 and noncurrent liability of $503,911", "Current liability of $96,000 and noncurrent liability of $499,340"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 842-20-45-1, the current portion of an operating lease liability equals the present value of lease payments due within the next 12 months. The PV of the next $96,000 payment at 5% is $91,429; the remainder ($595,340 − $91,429 = $503,911) is noncurrent. Choice D is incorrect because the current portion is the discounted amount of the next payment, not the undiscounted $96,000 nominal value.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Workers'' compensation self-insurance: IBNR evaluation
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Ironclad Manufacturing self-insures its workers'' compensation program. At December 31, 2025, known open claims total $340,000 and actuarial analysis estimates IBNR (incurred but not reported) claims at $185,000. The claims administrator recommends accruing only the $340,000, arguing that IBNR claims are too uncertain to record. Evaluate whether the company should follow the administrator''s recommendation.', '["Yes, because IBNR claims are speculative and should not be accrued until reported", "Yes, but IBNR should be disclosed in the footnotes as a reasonably possible contingency", "No, the IBNR should be accrued separately as a contingent liability at its maximum potential amount", "No, the total accrual should be $525,000 because both known and IBNR claims represent probable and estimable obligations"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 450-20-25 and ASC 954-450, an entity must accrue a liability when a loss is probable and reasonably estimable. Actuarially determined IBNR claims meet both criteria — the injuries have occurred even though reporting is pending. The total accrual is $340,000 + $185,000 = $525,000. Choice A is incorrect because IBNR claims are not speculative; they represent losses already incurred that are statistically measurable through actuarial methods.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: easy / L2 — Accrued commissions payable
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Drake Electronics pays its sales staff a 4% commission on all sales. December 2025 sales total $320,000 and commissions are paid on the 15th of the following month. What entry should Drake record at December 31, 2025?', '["No entry until commissions are paid on January 15", "Debit Commission Expense $12,800 and credit Accrued Commissions Payable $12,800", "Debit Prepaid Commissions $12,800 and credit Cash $12,800", "Debit Commission Expense $12,800 and credit Sales Revenue $12,800"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 340-40 and the accrual basis of accounting, commission expense is recognized in the period the related sales occur because the obligation arises when employees earn the commission. Drake accrues $320,000 × 4% = $12,800 as commission expense with a corresponding accrued liability. Choice A is incorrect because waiting until payment would understate both expenses and liabilities for the period in which the commissions were earned.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Vendor rebates: effect on payables and inventory
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Oakmont Hardware purchases $500,000 of inventory from a supplier during 2025. The supplier offers a 3% volume rebate if annual purchases exceed $400,000. By November 30, Oakmont has purchased $480,000 and in December purchases an additional $20,000, triggering the rebate. Analyze the effect on Oakmont''s December 31, 2025 balance sheet.', '["Accounts payable is reduced by $15,000 and inventory cost is reduced by $15,000", "A rebate receivable of $15,000 is recorded as other income", "No adjustment until the rebate check is received from the supplier", "Accounts payable is reduced by $14,400 and cost of goods sold is reduced by $14,400"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 705-20, volume rebates from a supplier are treated as a reduction of the purchase price, not as income. The rebate is $500,000 × 3% = $15,000, which reduces both accounts payable (or creates a receivable) and inventory cost (or COGS if goods are sold). Choice B is incorrect because ASC 705-20-25 requires vendor rebates to reduce the cost of purchases, not to be classified as revenue or other income.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Deferred compensation: estimate change evaluation
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Crestview Financial has an individual deferred compensation agreement with its CEO promising a lump-sum payment of $800,000 at retirement, originally estimated in 10 years. The CEO has completed 6 years of service as of December 31, 2025, and Crestview has accrued $480,000 ($80,000 per year). The CEO now announces she may retire in 8 total years instead of 10, leaving a remaining $320,000 to be accrued over 2 years instead of 4. Evaluate the correct treatment.', '["Continue accruing $80,000 annually because the original estimate should not change until retirement is certain", "Reverse prior accruals and re-spread the $800,000 over the revised 8-year period", "Recognize the remaining $320,000 over the revised 2-year service period at $160,000 per year prospectively", "Recognize the full remaining $320,000 immediately as a catch-up adjustment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 710-10-25 and ASC 250-10-45, a change in the estimated service period for deferred compensation is a change in accounting estimate applied prospectively. The remaining unaccrued balance of $320,000 ($800,000 − $480,000) is spread over the revised 2-year remaining period = $160,000 per year. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 250 requires entities to revise estimates when new information indicates a change, not to defer revisions until the event is certain.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Coupons and rebates outstanding: liability recognition
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'How should outstanding manufacturer coupons that have been issued but not yet redeemed be reported?', '["As a selling expense only when redeemed by customers", "As an estimated liability for the expected redemption amount", "As a contingent asset", "As a reduction of sales revenue"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 606-10-32-25, an entity accrues an estimated liability for outstanding coupons based on the expected redemption rate at the time of issuance. This matches the cost of the promotion with the revenue it generates. Choice A is incorrect because accrual accounting requires the obligation to be recognized when coupons are distributed, not when they are redeemed by customers.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Litigation settlement payable: classification
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'On November 1, 2025, Redfield Industries agrees to settle a product liability lawsuit for $275,000, payable in two installments: $137,500 on March 15, 2026, and $137,500 on March 15, 2027. The settlement agreement is signed on November 10. What liability should Redfield report at December 31, 2025?', '["$137,500 as a current liability only", "No liability until the first payment is due on March 15, 2026", "$275,000 as a current liability", "$275,000 total with $137,500 current and $137,500 noncurrent"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 450-20-25, once a settlement is agreed upon and signed, the loss is certain and must be accrued for the full amount. The $275,000 is classified by maturity: $137,500 due March 2026 is current, and $137,500 due March 2027 is noncurrent. Choice A is incorrect because both installments represent existing obligations even though the second payment extends beyond one year.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Due to related parties: classification
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Mercer Holdings lends $200,000 to its wholly owned subsidiary, Lakeview Properties, on September 1, 2025, with repayment due on August 31, 2026. On Lakeview''s December 31, 2025 standalone balance sheet, how should the $200,000 be classified?', '["As a current liability due to related party", "As a noncurrent liability because the parent will likely extend the term", "As a reduction of equity since the parent is the lender", "As an intercompany payable netted against Lakeview''s receivables from Mercer"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 850-10-50, related-party transactions are reported at the amounts agreed upon between the parties. Lakeview classifies the $200,000 as a current liability because repayment is due within one year of the balance sheet date. The related-party nature requires disclosure but does not change classification. Choice B is incorrect because classification depends on the contractual maturity date, not on speculation about whether the parent may extend the term.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Returnable container deposits
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'When a company receives a cash deposit from a customer for a returnable shipping container, how is the deposit recorded?', '["As shipping revenue", "As a reduction of inventory", "As a current liability", "As a contra-asset"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 210-10-45, a returnable container deposit is an obligation to refund cash when the container is returned, classified as a current liability. The deposit does not represent revenue because the entity has a continuing obligation to return the funds. Choice A is incorrect because no service has been performed — the entity merely holds the customer''s money as security for the container''s return.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Unclaimed property / escheat liability
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'How should outstanding checks and customer credit balances be treated after the statutory escheat period expires?', '["Recognized as miscellaneous income", "Reversed against retained earnings", "Carried as a liability indefinitely", "Remitted to the applicable state government under escheat laws"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 405-20 and state escheat laws, entities holding dormant funds such as outstanding checks or customer credit balances must remit these amounts to the state after the statutory dormancy period. The liability transfers to the state rather than being extinguished. Choice A is incorrect because recognizing unclaimed property as income is generally improper — the obligation transfers from the payee to the state, not to the entity''s earnings.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — ARO accretion expense calculation
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Trident Mining recognized an asset retirement obligation of $1,200,000 on January 1, 2023, using a credit-adjusted risk-free rate of 6%. No revisions to the estimated obligation have occurred. What amount of accretion expense should Trident recognize for the year ended December 31, 2025?', '["$72,000", "$80,899", "$76,320", "$85,753"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 410-20-35-5, accretion expense is computed by multiplying the beginning-of-period ARO balance by the credit-adjusted risk-free rate. The ARO accretes: end of 2023 = $1,272,000, end of 2024 = $1,348,320. Year 2025 accretion = $1,348,320 × 6% = $80,899. Choice A ($72,000) is incorrect because it uses the original balance rather than the accreted balance, ignoring the compounding effect of accretion over prior years.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Accounts payable dispute: evaluation of evidence
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Sterling Distributors receives a $420,000 invoice from Apex Suppliers for a December 2025 shipment. Sterling''s receiving report shows $380,000 of goods were delivered, while Apex insists the full amount was shipped per its bill of lading. Sterling''s quality inspection log confirms acceptance of $380,000. The $40,000 discrepancy is unresolved at year-end and legal counsel advises the vendor claim is without merit. Evaluate the proper accounts payable balance at December 31, 2025.', '["$380,000 based on the goods actually received and accepted", "$420,000 because the vendor invoice is the controlling document", "$400,000 as a compromise between the two positions pending resolution", "No payable until the dispute is fully resolved"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 405-20, a payable is recorded for the amount of goods or services actually received. Sterling''s internal documentation (receiving report and inspection log) confirms acceptance of $380,000. The entity records its payable based on acknowledged obligations, not unsubstantiated vendor claims. Choice B is incorrect because a vendor''s invoice does not override the entity''s receiving records — accounts payable reflects goods accepted, not amounts billed, when a legitimate dispute exists.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — In-substance defeasance: derecognition rules (ASC 405-20)
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Northfield Corp. places $1,050,000 in U.S. Treasury securities in an irrevocable trust to service a $1,000,000 bond payable due in 5 years. The trust cash flows will cover all remaining principal and interest payments. Analyze whether Northfield may remove the bond payable from its balance sheet.', '["Yes, because the risk of nonpayment has been effectively eliminated", "Yes, because the trust assets exceed the bond obligation", "No, because in-substance defeasance does not qualify for derecognition under ASC 405-20-40", "No, but the bond payable must be reduced by the fair value of the trust assets"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 405-20-40, a liability is extinguished only when the debtor pays the creditor, is legally released, or the obligation expires. Placing assets in a trust (in-substance defeasance) does not meet these criteria because the entity remains the primary obligor. The bond payable stays on the balance sheet with disclosure. Choice A is incorrect because reducing economic risk does not satisfy the legal extinguishment requirements of ASC 405-20-40.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Payroll withholding liabilities
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Ashford Services has gross payroll of $250,000 for December 2025. Withholdings include federal income tax of $37,500, employee FICA of $19,125, state income tax of $10,000, and employee health insurance premiums of $8,400. What total current liability should Ashford record for amounts withheld from employees?', '["$250,000", "$56,625", "$66,625", "$75,025"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 710-10, amounts withheld from employee paychecks are liabilities of the employer until remitted. The total is $37,500 + $19,125 + $10,000 + $8,400 = $75,025. All withheld amounts — taxes and benefit premiums — create a payable because the employer holds these funds as agent. Choice C ($66,625) is incorrect because it omits the $8,400 health insurance premiums, which are also withheld amounts the employer must remit to the insurance carrier.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Contingent liability disclosure: reasonably possible
(2, 'Payables and Accrued Liabilities', 'Midland Technologies is defending a patent infringement lawsuit. Legal counsel advises that an unfavorable outcome is reasonably possible but not probable, with potential damages between $750,000 and $1,200,000. What is the appropriate treatment at December 31, 2025?', '["Accrue $750,000 as a loss contingency and disclose the range", "Disclose the nature of the litigation and the range of potential loss in the footnotes without accrual", "No disclosure or accrual is required for reasonably possible contingencies", "Accrue $975,000, the midpoint of the range, as a loss contingency"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 450-10-50-3, when a loss contingency is reasonably possible but not probable, the entity must disclose the nature of the contingency and an estimate of the possible loss or range in the footnotes. Accrual is not required because the probable threshold is not met. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 450-10-25 requires accrual only when the loss is probable and reasonably estimable — reasonably possible triggers disclosure, not recognition.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
